package diag

// Explain holds the one-paragraph explanation printed by `alder explain <code>`
// for every diagnostic code the compiler raises.
var Explain = map[string]string{
	"E0001": "Parse error. The file does not follow Alder's grammar (chapter 3 and appendix A): a token was found where the parser expected something else, a string or element was left open, a declaration is malformed, an identifier contains two underscores in a row (`a__b`), or a module name segment ends with an underscore. Parsing reports every error it finds, then the compiler stops before name resolution so one typo does not produce a cascade. Fix the first error and re-run; later ones are often consequences.",
	"E0002": "Unknown name. An identifier, constructor, type, module, or message was used but nothing with that name is in scope (chapters 3 and 6). Check the spelling, make sure the name is declared, and remember that messages of the App module are written `App.Logout` and must be declared with `msg` in `App.alder`. For a name that exists in another module, import it (E0201) or qualify the type (E0202). A bare name that is both a constructor and a table in scope (`Member` of `type Role = Reader | Member` and `table Member`) is the table where a `Table` is expected (the first argument of a `Db.` operation) and the constructor where its union is expected; anywhere else it is E0002 naming both: qualify the table (`Member.Member`), or use the name where the type says which (chapter 3).",
	"E0003": "Type mismatch. An expression has one type where the context needs another (chapter 4). Alder has no implicit conversions except widening a brand to its base type and a `requires` page's `Session` to the app's `Session` (never the reverse), so `Int` in a `Text` position needs `Text.fromInt`, `+` does not join text (use `++`), and a `Time` is not a `Date`. The message names both types; convert the value or change the signature. Signatures of `load`, `view`, `update`, `error`, and `notFound` have fixed shapes (chapter 6), and `view` is always `Model -> Session -> Html Msg`. A `Dict` key may not be or contain a function or an effect value (`Query`, `Task`), also inside a union constructor's payload, whether the `Dict` type is written or only inferred (`Dict.insert inc \"a\" Dict.empty`): keys are compared with `==`, which functions and effects do not have; use a name or an id as the key. For the same reason `==` and `!=` reject functions and effect values: run an effect with `let x <-` and compare its result. A column method (`.eq`, `.gt`, `.asc`, …) needs its column written in place as `Table.field` (a column cannot be stored in a variable), and a `Db` function needs a concrete table: `Table Todo`, not `Table t`.",
	"E0004": "Non-exhaustive match. A `match` does not cover every constructor of the union it inspects (chapter 4). Every case must be handled so that adding a constructor later shows exactly where code needs updating. Add the missing arms listed in the message, or end with a `_` arm if a default is really intended.",
	"E0005": "Module path mismatch. A module's name must match its file path: `app/Todos/Index.alder` declares `module Todos.Index` (chapter 3). Rename the module line or move the file so the two agree.",
	"E0006": "Import cycle. Module A imports B which imports A, directly or through other modules (chapter 3). Modules form a directed acyclic graph. Break the cycle by moving the shared declarations into a third module that both import.",
	"E0007": "Missing type signature. Every top-level value needs a signature on the line before its definition (chapter 3). Signatures are the checked documentation of a module and the contract other modules rely on. Write `name : Type` above the definition; the fix inserts the type the compiler infers from the body, such as `rank : Priority -> Int`, or `name : ?type` as a placeholder to complete when the type cannot be fully inferred (a polymorphic value, or a body with another error).",
	"E0008": "Arity error. A function is defined, applied, or passed with the wrong number of parameters (chapter 3). The definition must name exactly as many parameters as its signature's arrows, a lambda must match the function type expected, and a constructor with no payload takes no argument. A type takes exactly as many type arguments as it declares parameters: `type Box a = …` is written `Box Int`, never `Box` or `Box Int Text`. Add or remove the argument.",
	"E0009": "Duplicate definition. Two declarations in the same scope share a name, or two imported modules declare a constructor with the same name (chapters 3 and 4). Constructors share one unqualified namespace across imports, so they must be unique. A module M that declares a capability also named M may not declare a value with the name of one of its entries: `M.x` would name both. Rename one of them.",
	"E0010": "Missing required declaration. A module is missing something its role demands (chapter 6): a page module needs `load`, `view`, and `Model`, plus `update` if it declares any `msg`; `App` needs `error` and `notFound`, an `update` if it declares messages, and a `login` page and `session` if any page uses `requires`. Add the declaration named in the message.",
	"E0011": "Invalid declaration. The declaration is well-formed but breaks a rule of its kind (chapters 4, 6, and 8): a table needs exactly one `@id` field of a `brand Text` type, stored fields must be scalar, brands of scalars, or `Maybe` of those, page parameters and form fields must be decodable from URLs and forms, `requires` must name a `Maybe` session field, brand constructors may only be used in their own module or inside a `test`, a record may not contain itself by value (`type R = { r: R }`, directly or through other records: no value of it could ever be built) — reach it through `Maybe`, `List`, `Dict` or a union instead — and a delete rule (`@cascade`, `@restrict`, `@setNull`) belongs on a `@ref` column, after `@ref Table` (`post: Post.PostId @ref Post @cascade`), one per column, and `@renamed \"old\"` (on a table after its name, on a field after its type) names the table's or field's previous name: an identifier, not the current name, not a field the table still has, and one per table or field. Change the declaration as the message describes.",
	"E0014": "Module names that differ only in case. Two modules are named alike except for upper- and lower-case letters (`Ab` and `AB`, `Todo.Item` and `Todo.ITEM`). Each module becomes a file of the compiled program named after it, and on a case-insensitive file system (the default on macOS and Windows) the two would be the same file; their source files would clash the same way. Rename one of the modules (and its file) so the names differ in more than case.",
	"E0015": "Reserved module name: a module's first segment may not be exactly `Route`, `Cap` or `Args` (so `Route`, `Cap.Mail` and `Args.X` are refused; `Routes`, `Capx` and `My.Cap` are fine). The compiled program has exported names that start with `Route__`, `Cap__` and `Args__` (the routes, and the Go interfaces and argument records of capabilities that the adapters implement), and a module under one of those names could give a declaration the same Go name. Rename the module (and its file), for example `Routing` or `Caps.Mail`.",
	"E0017": "A module named like a standard-library module. The standard library's modules (`List`, `Text`, `Http`, `Test`, …) are always in scope under their names, so an app module `Test` would be shadowed by the stdlib's: `Test.helper` would be looked up in the stdlib and not found. The module is refused (and its uses are not reported separately). Rename the module and its file, for example `TestHelpers` or `Todos.Fixtures` (chapter 3, \"Modules\").",
	"E0018": "A table declared outside its own module. A table is declared in the module named after it, with the brand for its id and its functions (chapter 8): `table Comment` belongs in `app/Comment.alder` (module `Comment`), not in `app/Post.alder`. A table's columns are named through its module (`Comment.post`), so a table declared anywhere else could not be queried from another module. The error is reported once, at the declaration; uses of the table in other modules point back at it. Move the table, its id brand and its functions to their own module and `import Comment` where it is used.",
	"E0019": "The app's tables changed since its last migration. An app with a `migrations/` directory keeps its database in step with its `table` declarations through migration files: `migrations/schema.json` records the tables as the last migration left them, and `alder check` compares it with the tables the app declares now. The message names the first table that differs (`table Post: column body`). A database reaches the new tables only through a new migration, so `alder build`, `run`, `test` and `eval` refuse the app until you run `alder migrate`, which writes the next `migrations/NNNN_<name>.sql` (read it: a line `-- DROPS DATA` says which rows or values the change loses) and updates `schema.json`; commit both with the change. A changed field default alone is not a change: a default is not in the database's tables. E0019 also reports `migrations/` disagreeing with itself — a file missing or edited (restore `migrations/` from version control), two files with one number from two branches, a storage-format change — and its message names the remedy.",
	"E0020": "A `fake` declared outside its capability's module. A fake is an in-memory implementation of a whole capability for tests (chapter 10): `fake Stripe { … }` holds tables of its own and one definition per entry of `capability Stripe`, each checked against the entry's type. It is declared beside the capability, in the module that declares `capability Stripe` (`app/Payments.alder`), so the capability, its real adapter's contract and its fake are read together, and a capability has one fake (a second is E0009). Move the `fake` declaration into that module. A test anywhere installs it with `let _ <- Test.fake Stripe`.",
	"E0021": "A fake's table used outside tests and the fake. The tables declared inside `fake Stripe { … }` exist only in test databases: they are in no migration and no `schema.json`, and a production binary has no such table. So only a `test` body (`Db.from Payments.Charge`) and the fake's own entries may use one; a value, a page, a type or a signature of the app may not, and neither may an app table's `@ref` (an app table never refers to a fake's; a fake's table may refer to an app table). Move the code that reads the table into a test, or, if the app needs the data, declare an app table in its own module.",
	"E0022": "A table field's default is not a literal. A table field's default (`role: Role = Reader`) is written into the database by migrations (`alder migrate`: a new non-null column is filled with it, and a column that stops being `Maybe` replaces NULL with it), so it must be a value SQL can hold as written: `True` or `False`, an integer, a float, a string without `{…}`, a constructor without arguments (`Reader`, `Nothing`), or `Just` of one of those. An expression (`count: Int = 1 + 1`, `title: Text = defaultTitle`) is refused; write its value instead. Defaults of forms, records, the session and page parameters are not stored, and stay free to be any expression (chapter 8).",
	"E0023": "Not a selector. The queries of module Test (`Test.text`, `texts`, `has`, `count` and `attr`) find elements of a page's document by a selector, a small subset of CSS's: a tag (`li`, or a custom element with a hyphen), `#id`, `.class`, `[attr]` and `[attr=value]` (the value a word or a quoted string), combined into one compound (`li.done`, `input[name=title]`) and joined by spaces (a descendant: `ul li`) or `>` (a child: `ul > li`). A selector written as a literal is checked when the test is: the message names the token that does not fit (`#sumary x`: `x` is not a tag). Fix the selector. A selector that is not a literal (built from text at run time) is not checked; one that does not parse matches nothing, so the query gives `Nothing`, `[]`, `False` or `0`.",
	"E0024": "An `@owner` column that is not one. `@owner` marks the column of a table that says whose row it is (chapter 6, \"Rules\"): `owner: User.UserId @ref User @owner`. It must be a `@ref` column whose type is the session's user type (with `session { user: Maybe User.UserId }`, the column's type is `User.UserId`, not `Maybe`), since the scoped operations compare it with `session.user`; a table has at most one such column; and the app's session must have a `user` field. The message says which rule the declaration breaks. Mark the column that holds the user's id, remove a second `@owner`, or give App a `session { user: Maybe User.UserId }`. `@owner` changes nothing in the database: it is a marker on a column that is already there, and `alder migrate` ignores it.",
	"E0025": "A scoped operation on a table without `@owner`. `Db.mine`, `Db.getMine`, `Db.updateMine`, `Db.deleteMine` and `Db.insertMine` keep to the rows of the signed-in user by comparing the table's `@owner` column with `session.user`; a table without one has nothing to compare. Mark the column holding the user's id `@owner` (`owner: User.UserId @ref User @owner`), or use the unscoped operation (`Db.from`, `Db.get`, `Db.update`, `Db.delete`, `Db.insert`) and write the check yourself.",
	"E0026": "A scoped operation where there is no signed-in user. The scoped operations (`Db.mine`, `Db.getMine`, `Db.updateMine`, `Db.deleteMine`, `Db.insertMine`) compare a table's `@owner` column with `session.user`, so they compile only where `session.user` is certainly there: in a page module whose page `requires user` — its `load`, `update`, `view` and helper values — and in tests (a test signs in with `Test.login`; a scoped operation run with no user signed in fails). App's `update`, a table's module, any other module and a page without `requires user` have no such user, and neither does a `fake`. A value of a `requires user` page module that uses a scoped operation (itself or through the module's other values) carries the rule with it: referring to it from such a place is E0026 too, naming the value and the operation. Add `requires user` to the page, move the code into such a page module, or use the unscoped operation with an explicit check.",
	"E0027": "A `role` requirement that cannot be checked. `requires user, role Author` (or `role Author | Admin`) lets a page answer only a signed-in user whose session's `role` is one of the named constructors; any other user gets the not-found page (chapter 6, \"Rules\"). It needs `user` first (`requires role Author` alone is refused), and App's session needs a field named `role` whose type is a union (not `Maybe`) with those constructors: `session { user: Maybe User.UserId, role: User.Role = Reader }`, set at login with `Session.put`. The role is read from the session cookie, so a change of role applies at the next login: a role in the cookie lasts until logout or the session's 30-day lifetime from login (visits do not renew it). A page where a demotion must apply at once also re-reads the user's row in `load` (the blog's admin pages do).",
	"E0016": "Two pages answer the same path. Each `page \"…\"` declaration is one URL pattern, and two pages whose patterns differ only in the names of their `{params}` (`/x/{id}` and `/x/{slug}`) or a trailing slash would answer the same requests: only one could ever be served (chapter 6). Give one of them another path, such as `/x/by-slug/{slug}`. Patterns that merely overlap are fine: a literal segment wins over a parameter (`/x/new` is served before `/x/{id}`, whatever the module order).",
	"E0012": "Reserved name. The compiler generates some types in a page module and in App: `Params` (a page's parameters), `Msg` (the messages), `Session` (the session the module's code sees) and, for every `msg Pick`, the record `PickParams` of its parameters (chapter 6). A type, table, form or component of that module may not take one of these names, because it would be the same name as the generated type in the compiled program. Rename your declaration, for example `PickArgs` instead of `PickParams`. In every app module, a type, table, form or union may not take the name of a Prelude type that programs write either (`Query`, `Task`, `Html`, `Maybe`, `Result`, `Nav`, `List`, `Dict`, `Int`, `Float`, `Text`, `Bool`, `Unit`, `Date`, `Time`, `Never`, `Error`, and the test types `Doc` and `Answer`): every use of the name in that module, such as `load`'s `Query Model`, would read as your declaration. Likewise no constructor or `msg` may take the name of a constructor of the test type `Answer` (`Shown`, `Rerendered`, `Redirected`, `FormRejected`, `PageMissing`, `RequestFailed`): inside a `test` it would hide the Prelude's. The Prelude's plumbing types (`Order`, `Page`, `Form`, `Table`, `Id`, `Insert`, `Select`, `Condition`, `Column`) are not reserved: `table Order` is fine. Until it is renamed (`form SearchForm` instead of `form Query`), the module's other errors are not reported, since they would only be consequences of the name.",
	"E0028": "A custom element the checker cannot vouch for. A tag with a hyphen (`<al-button>`, `<wa-button>`) is a custom element, checked against Custom Elements Manifests (chapter 5, \"Custom elements\"): the `alder-ui` design system's, built into the compiler, and the app's own `elements/custom-elements.json`, which declares any other (Web Awesome's manifest, copied there, for example); for a tag both declare, the app's entry wins. An element no manifest declares is refused, as is an attribute its entry does not list (except `id`, `class`, `style`, `slot`, `hidden`, `title`, `lang`, `dir`, `tabindex`, `role`, `data-*`, `aria-*` and Alder's `click` and `href`; on `al-input`, `al-textarea`, `al-select` and `al-checkbox` also `field` and `value`), a value written for a boolean attribute (write `disabled` or `disabled={expr}`), a literal value outside an attribute's declared set (`variant=\"destuctive\"`; a computed value is not checked), `submit` on any custom element (it belongs on `<form>`), and a `slot=\"x\"` naming a slot its parent does not declare, or written on a child of a plain element (`<div>`) instead of the custom element's own child. An `<al-button>` given `click`, `type` or `href` renders its own `<button>` or `<a>`, so it takes `href` or `click`, not both, and its children hold no control (`<button>`, `<a>`, `<input>`, `<select>`, `<textarea>`, an element with `click`) and no `Html` value or component: text and inline markup only. An `<Errors field>` for a field an `al-input` (or textarea, select, checkbox) already shows is refused too. The message lists what the entry declares. A manifest file that does not parse is reported at the file, with the reason.",
	"E0029": "A page path the router cannot serve. A `page \"…\"` path is one `/` followed by segments, each of letters, digits, `.`, `_`, `~` and `-`, or a whole `{param}` (`/posts/{id}/edit`); a trailing `/` is allowed (the router serves `/x/` as `/x`). Anything else checks as text but answers no request, or the wrong one: an empty segment (`//evil.com` is read by a browser as another host), a space, a query (`/a?b=1`: a page's query parameters are its `{ … }` fields that no `{param}` segment names), a fragment (`#`), a quote or a backslash, a segment `.` or `..` (a browser resolves them away), or a parameter inside a segment (`/a{id}`). Write the path with plain segments; `alder new page` refuses the same paths.",
	"E0101": "`Db.get` takes the table's id type. `Db.get Post params.slug` fails because `slug` is not a `PostId`; `Db.get` looks a row up by primary key only (chapter 8). To look up by another column, query: `Db.from Post |> Db.where (Post.slug.eq slug) |> Db.first`, which returns a `Maybe Post`.",
	"E0102": "Field access on a `Maybe`. A value that may be absent, such as the result of `Db.get`, `Db.first`, or a `Maybe` session field, cannot be read with `.field` until it has been matched (chapters 4 and 8). Use `match value { Just v -> ..., Nothing -> ... }` and read the field from `v`; the fix inserts the skeleton.",
	"E0201": "Module not imported. A qualified name such as `Todo.Priority` or `Db.from Todo` refers to a module this file has not imported (chapter 3). Add `import Todo` after the `module` line. Standard-library modules such as `Db`, `List`, and `Text` are always available; pages and App messages are global. When the module already imports this file, directly or through others (`Theme` imports `Util`, and `Util` uses `Theme.name`), the import would be a cycle (E0006) and cannot be added; the message names the chain. Move the code that uses the module's names into that module, or, for a `test`, write the test in that module's file.",
	"E0202": "Unqualified type from another module. A type declared in another module must be written with its module name, `Todo.Priority`, not `Priority` (chapters 3 and 4). Constructors, by contrast, are always unqualified. Qualify the type; the fix shows how.",
	"E0203": "Qualified constructor. Constructors are never qualified: write `Normal`, not `Todo.Normal` (chapter 4). Only types and values are qualified by their module. Remove the module prefix.",
	"E0301": "Message sent without its parameters. A `msg` declared with parameters, such as `msg Toggle { id: TodoId }`, must be sent with a record: `click={Toggle { id: todo.id }}` (chapter 6). A bare `Toggle` is a function, not a message. The fix provides the record with a `?hole` for each field.",
	"E0302": "`update` does not handle every message. The `update` of a page must have an arm for every `msg` the page declares (chapter 6), so a message can never be sent that nothing handles. Add the arms listed in the message; the fix inserts a skeleton.",
	"E0303": "`href` needs a page value. Links are checked: `href` takes a page applied to its parameters, such as `href={Todos.Index {}}`, or `Url.external \"https://...\"` for an outside address (chapter 5). A plain string could point at a route that does not exist.",
	"E0304": "Form input problem. `<Input>`, `<Textarea>`, `<Select>`, and `<Errors>` are bound to the form type of the message in the enclosing `<form submit={...}>` (chapters 5 and 6). `field` must be a literal that names a field of that form (the message must be declared with `form F`), and the component must suit the field's type: `<Select>` for unions, `<Input>` otherwise. A plain `<input>` inside such a form is an error. The message lists the form's fields.",
	"E0310": "Unreachable match arm. An arm can never run because earlier arms already handle every case it could match, for example a repeated constructor or a catch-all after full coverage (chapter 4). Delete the arm.",
	"E0401": "Write inside a read-only context. `load` and `Query` blocks can only read; `Db.insert`, `Db.update`, `Db.delete`, and other `Task` operations belong in `update` (chapters 7 and 8). This keeps page loads free of side effects. Move the write to `update` and return the result there.",
	"E0402": "`let x <-` outside an effect block. The bind arrow runs an effect and is only allowed in a block whose result is a `Query` or a `Task` (chapter 7). In a plain block use `let x = ...`; if you meant to run an effect, make the block's type `Query a` or `Task a`.",
	"E0501": "`Db.insert` never takes a generated column. The `@id`, `@created`, and `@updated` fields of a table are generated by the database layer, so the record given to `Db.insert` must leave them out (chapter 8). Remove the field from the record literal.",
	"E0502": "Wrong `@ref` type. A field declared `@ref Table` must have the id type of that table, or `Maybe` of it, such as `owner: User.UserId @ref User` (chapter 8). Using the table's other columns or a made-up id brand is an error. Change the field type to the referenced table's id.",
	"E0601": "`Msg` outside a page module. `Msg` is generated for each page module and for `App`; a shared module such as a layout or component library has no `Msg` of its own (chapters 5 and 6). Type shared components as `Html a`, so that the caller decides the message type. The fix replaces `Msg` with `a`.",
	"E0602": "Syntax from another language. The parser recognized a construct that Alder deliberately does not have (chapter 3): `case x of` (use `match x { ... }`), `let ... in` (use a block `{ let y = 1 ... }`), a tuple `(a, b)` (use a record `{ a: 1, b: 2 }`), or a type alias such as `type alias P = …` or `type Model = Unit` (a new name is always a new type: declare a record or a union with its own constructors). Rewrite it with the Alder form named in the message.",
	"E0030": "A `deploy` declaration `alder deploy` cannot use (chapter 16). `deploy { … }` appears at most once, in `App`; it is data and changes nothing in the program. An environment is a field whose value is a target: `Ssh \"user@host\" { domain: \"…\", path: \"…\" }` (optional `user`, `port`), `Fly \"region\" { app: \"…\" }` or `Script \"./path\"`; other kinds are unknown. Each target needs its fields (`domain` and `path` for Ssh, `app` for Fly). An Ssh target's texts are held to what they can be, because they reach ssh's command line, shell scripts and a systemd unit: the host is letters, digits, `.`, `-`, `_`, `@` (and `:`, `[`, `]`), the user letters, digits, `.`, `-`, `_`, the domain letters, digits, `.`, `-` (optionally starting `*.`), none of them beginning with `-`; the path is absolute and has no control character, `\"`, `\\`, `$`, `` ` `` or `%`. `floor` is a mutation score between 0 and 1; `keep` a whole number; `checks` and `requires` lists of text, no name empty; `backup` is `S3 \"s3://…\"`; `proxy` is `Caddy` or `None`; an environment is declared once. The file carries no secret: a text that looks like one (a URL with `user:password@`, a key beginning `sk_`, `ghp_`, `AKIA`, `xoxb-`, `xoxp-` or `-----BEGIN`, or 32 or more base64 or hex characters without a space) is refused. Name the variable in `requires` and set it in the target's environment instead.",
	"W0006": "An adapter reads an environment variable that `deploy.requires` does not name. The checker scans the Go packages under `adapters/` for the text given to `os.Getenv` and `os.LookupEnv` and compares it with the `requires` list of `App`'s `deploy` declaration (chapter 16): a variable read but not listed would be missing at the target without `alder deploy` noticing before the switch. Add the name to `requires`. A name in `requires` that no adapter reads raises nothing (the runtime may read it), and the runtime's own `ALDER_…` variables are never reported.",
	"W0001": "Unused import. The module is imported but none of its names are used in this file (chapter 3). The program is still valid; remove the `import` line to keep the module graph honest.",
	"W0003": "A form field's errors would not be shown. When a submitted form does not decode, the page is rendered again with each field's errors in its `<Errors field=\"…\">` and the errors of fields without one in the form's `<Errors form />` (chapter 5). This form has neither for the field, so a user who gets it wrong sees the form come back with no reason. Add `<Errors field=\"x\" />` next to the field's input, or `<Errors form />` to the form. A `Bool` field (a checkbox) never fails to decode and is not reported.",
	"W0005": "A rejection the user would never see. When a page's `update` answers `Reject \"…\"` for a message, the page is rendered again with that text in the posting form's `<Errors form />` (chapter 6). This form posts a message whose arm of `update` can answer `Reject`, but it has no `<Errors form />`, so the page comes back looking unchanged and the user is not told why (a browser test sees `Rerendered`, not `Rejected`). Add `<Errors form />` to the form, usually as its first child.",
	"W0004": "A default on a plain record type never applies. A field default (`loud: Bool = False`) fills the field where a value crosses a boundary: an insert of a `table`'s row, the session cookie, a page's or a message's parameters, a submitted `form` (chapter 8), and the literals of an anonymous record type such as a component's props. A literal of a type declared with `type R = { … }` names every field, so a default there never applies to anything. Remove it, or make the type anonymous (write the record type where it is used) if its literals should be able to leave the field out.",
	"W0002": "Typed hole. `?name` marks a place where an expression is still to be written (chapter 11). The program is checked with the hole present and the warning reports the type the hole must have, so a person or an agent can fill it in. Replace `?name` with an expression of that type.",
}
